# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# TSan decides two accesses are unsynchronised by tracking the happens-before
# edges it observes. It sees edges from pthread primitives even inside
# uninstrumented libraries, because those are interceptors — but it CANNOT see
# ordering established by lock-free atomics compiled into a library it did not
# instrument. Qt is consumed prebuilt here, and its future/result plumbing and
# thread pool are built on exactly such atomics, so TSan reports races that the
# code does not have.
#
# This is not a theoretical concern. On the first full run of the lane, 338 data
# races were reported across the PJ4 suite and the racing frame resolved as:
#
#     214  libQt6Core          84  TSan's own allocator interceptor, inside
#      28  libglib-2.0             Qt's QFuture/QtConcurrent machinery
#       6  FFmpeg               6  PJ4's own instrumented code
#
# Six of 338 pointed at code we compile. A signal that weak is not a signal, so
# the lane needed these suppressions to be worth reading at all.
#
# THE LIMITATION, STATED PLAINLY
#
# called_from_lib is a blunt instrument: it drops a report when the named
# library appears in the stack, so a GENUINE race in PJ4 code reached through a
# Qt callback or a queued slot is suppressed along with the noise. That is a
# real loss of coverage, accepted because the alternative — 300+ reports nobody
# reads — finds nothing at all.
#
# The only complete fix is an instrumented Qt, i.e. building Qt from source
# under -fsanitize=thread. That is the same "instrumented dependency closure"
# requirement that puts MemorySanitizer out of scope (see docs/SANITIZERS.md),
# and it is not free: it would add a from-source Qt to every TSan run.
#
# Add an entry only with a report that justifies it, and say which library the
# racing frame resolved to. A race in code we compile is the thing this lane
# exists to find: name PJ4 code only for a hand-off through Qt, audited as the
# section at the end of this file describes.

# Qt: prebuilt and uninstrumented, every module of it.
#
# Each module is named in full, and that is forced by the tool rather than a
# style choice. A called_from_lib pattern must resolve to exactly ONE loaded
# library; a prefix like "libQt6" matches several and TSan treats that as fatal:
#
#   ThreadSanitizer: called_from_lib suppression 'libQt6' is matched against
#   2 libraries: '...libQt6Svg.so.6' and '...libQt6Concurrent.so.6'
#
# The binary then exits 66, which breaks gtest_discover_tests and takes the
# entire app stage down with it — a broad pattern here costs the whole suite,
# not just a noisy report. The ".so" suffix keeps each entry unambiguous, since
# it stops "libQt6Qml.so" from also matching "libQt6QmlCore.so.6".
#
# This is the set PJ4 and its tests actually load, taken from ldd rather than
# from the 71 modules the Qt installation ships.
called_from_lib:libQt6Core.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Gui.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Widgets.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Concurrent.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Network.so
called_from_lib:libQt6DBus.so
called_from_lib:libQt6OpenGL.so
called_from_lib:libQt6OpenGLWidgets.so
called_from_lib:libQt6PrintSupport.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Svg.so
called_from_lib:libQt6SvgWidgets.so
called_from_lib:libQt6UiTools.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Xml.so

# GLib: system library reached through Qt's Unix event dispatcher.
called_from_lib:libglib-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libgobject-2.0.so

# FFmpeg: prebuilt Conan package with its own worker threads.
called_from_lib:libavutil.so
called_from_lib:libavcodec.so
called_from_lib:libavformat.so
called_from_lib:libavfilter.so
called_from_lib:libswscale.so
called_from_lib:libswresample.so

# Hand-offs through Qt's thread pool and event queue.
#
# QtConcurrent::run builds its task object (vptr, captured values) on the calling
# thread and QThreadPool hands it to a worker. A queued call builds its event
# (QMetaCallEvent, QCallableObject, copied arguments) on the posting thread and
# the receiving thread unpacks it. Both hand-offs are ordered by a QMutex inside
# libQt6Core, a futex TSan cannot see, so the first access on the other thread
# is reported against the construction. The called_from_lib entries above do not
# cover these: the racing frames are Qt header templates instantiated in our
# binaries, and our own lambdas. An instrumented Qt would make every one of them
# unnecessary.
#
# Each site was audited against its report (2026-09-14): values are captured by
# copy, the object is used on one thread at a time, and results return through a
# QFutureWatcher or a queued call on the GUI thread. A per-site entry names the Qt
# wrapper instantiated for one lambda (StoredFunctionCall for a task,
# QCallableObject for a queued call), so it matches only reports with a frame
# running inside that task or queued call: a genuine race in that lambda's body
# is hidden too, the rest of the submitting function is not. Re-audit a site when
# its threading changes, and add a new site only with a report and the same audit.
#
# Top frame in the task object or the queued-call glue, whichever site built it.
race_top:QtConcurrent::RunFunctionTaskBase
race_top:qcoreevent.h
race_top:qobjectdefs_impl.h
# QtConcurrent tasks: the captured values, first read on the worker. The MeshLoader
# entry covers load() and loadFromMemory(), reached from scene entities, robot
# models and the loader itself.
race:QtConcurrent::StoredFunctionCall<pj::scene3d::MeshLoader::load
race:QtConcurrent::StoredFunctionCall<pj::scene3d::PointCloudLayer::startDecode
race:QtConcurrent::StoredFunctionCall<pj::scene3d::TrailLayer::startRebuild
race:QtConcurrent::StoredFunctionCall<PJ::DownloadManager::onReplyFinished
# Queued lambdas posted from provider, ingest or plugin worker threads: their
# captures, first read on the GUI thread.
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::HeadlessDescriptorProviderSession::startImport
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::FileLoader::beginLoad
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::FileLoader::runIngestOnWorker
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::ToolboxRuntimeHost::onCreateParserIngest
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::ToolboxRuntimeHost::postIngestFinished
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::ToolboxRuntimeHost::onNotifyDataChanged
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::wireSourceCapture
# The plugin message-box request, posted by the ingest worker, which then blocks
# until the GUI thread answers it. The * spans "(anonymous namespace)::".
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::*makePluginMessageBoxHandler
# A finished-slot object the GUI thread ran, freed later by Qt's network thread.
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject<PJ::*attachFinished

# Test plugin libraries are dlclosed between tests and reloaded at the same
# address. TSan keeps the unloaded library's access history, so the next load's
# one-time guarded init of the plugin vtable is reported against a worker of the
# previous load that has already exited. The table is written once per load and
# only read afterwards.
race:PJ::DataSourcePluginBase::vtableWithCreate
